WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:21.280
I'm exploring the fascinating world of plants, from the most bizarre to the most beautiful.

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:32.240
With new techniques, we can unravel their deepest secrets.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:41.120
We can move from our time scale to theirs.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:45.280
We can see how they struggle with one another.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:53.280
How they get help from some animals, but have to battle with others.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.280
And survive against the odds.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:07.280
And we can watch all these dramas taking place in a way that is impossible in the wild in one unique institution.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:11.280
The Royal Botanic Gardens at Kew.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:21.280
In this living laboratory, we can discover plants' most extreme adaptations for survival.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:49.280
And we can glimpse Kew's tiny weapons in the battle to protect the future of all plants, seeds.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:59.280
Ever since plants first moved out of water and colonized the land, they've advanced into drier and drier places.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:05.280
And evolved specialist adaptations in order that they can do so.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:11.280
Today, deserts cover about a third of the land surface of the earth, and they're spreading rapidly.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:21.280
But some plants have developed some truly extraordinary strategies to enable them to survive in the driest of places.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:57.280
In deserts where water is scarce, one major strategy is to remain passive during the day when temperatures soar, and only become active in the cool of the night.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:11.280
This desert, in Kew's Princess of Wales Conservatory, contains plants from arid regions all around the world.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:21.280
And in midsummer, one plant here does something truly astonishing.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:27.280
Don't be deceived by the shriveled, untidy appearance of this cactus.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:32.280
It is in fact one of the most remarkable members of the cactus family.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:37.280
It's called Hyla Sirius, the Queen of the Night.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:46.280
It lives in Mexico, where during the summer at midday, the temperatures can reach a crippling 50 degrees centigrade.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:51.280
So during the day, it doesn't do much.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:04:20.280
But once a year, and for one night only, it becomes a thing of astounding beauty.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:28.280
The flowers usually open on a night of the full moon.

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:35.280
And as they do so, they give off a smell which attracts night-flying animals, large and small.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:41.280
And as they get nearer, they can see the great white flowers bright in the light of the full moon.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:52.280
And they go to them to sip the nectar, and in doing so, pollinate the cactus.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:05:02.280
It might seem strange, even risky, for a plant to restrict its flowering to just one night of the year.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:07.280
But this strategy works for Hyla Sirius.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:12.280
Its flowers are the biggest and brightest around.

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:18.280
And they offer its pollinators a nectar bonanza.

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:21.280
Here are the biggest.

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:31.280
Bats.

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:37.280
Bats are a vital part of many desert ecosystems.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:50.280
They pollinate all kinds of cacti.

00:05:50.280 --> 00:06:15.280
In springtime in South American deserts, there is always a cactus of one kind or another in bloom.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:27.280
A multitude of species compete for the attentions of nocturnal pollinators using bright petals and powerful scent.

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:35.280
The sweetest perfumes attract insects.

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:43.280
But flowers that are pollinated by bats are rather more pungent.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:54.280
They give off the stench of rotten fruit.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:07:00.280
To nectar-feeding bats, that smell is irresistible.

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:10.280
They fly from cactus to cactus, sipping up the nectar.

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:22.280
A bat can consume one and a half times its body weight of nectar in a single night.

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:35.280
Very tall columnar species produce flowers right at the very top of their stems, where they're easily smelt and seen and easily reached.

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:40.280
Some provide additional facilities.

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:53.280
Espastoa guantaray from Bolivia sprouts a landing strip beneath its flowers, a pad of fur on which the bats can alight without damaging their wings on the spines.

00:07:53.280 --> 00:08:01.280
But in Mexico, bats and cacti have an even more extraordinary relationship.

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:11.280
Each year, bats migrate across a thousand miles of desert from central Mexico to Arizona.

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:21.280
Pregnant females, 100,000 of them, are on their way to special maternity caves in the north where they will give birth to their young.

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:30.280
The bats get all the nectar they need to sustain their journey, and in return, they pollinate the cacti.

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:48.280
This annual bat migration coincides exactly with the blooming of the night-flowering cacti, so the bats can fly along a nectar corridor

00:08:48.280 --> 00:09:03.280
and so can cross a patch of desert that otherwise, and at other times, would be impossible to them.

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:12.280
By the time the bats return with their pups, the cacti are able once again to provide the bats with food, this time their fruits.

00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:24.280
The bats carry with them in their stomachs the fruit and the seed it contains, and in due course, they deposit those seeds with a nice little packet of fertilizer farther down the corridor.

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:43.280
So the corridors are self-perpetuating. The bats, in effect, are cactus farmers.

00:09:54.280 --> 00:10:20.280
Perhaps the most extraordinary thing about these night-blooming cacti is that just a few hours after the flowers burst open, they begin to close.

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:29.280
By the time the sun appears, they have already wilted and died.

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:35.280
This is all part of their survival strategy.

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:57.280
The closed petals form a protective shield around the newly pollinated seeds, locking vital moisture inside.

00:10:57.280 --> 00:11:11.280
The light of a new day reveals the bizarre nature of dry zone plants.

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:26.280
These are surely some of the oddest looking species there are, and their strange shapes are a direct response to the harsh conditions.

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:38.280
These plants must stay in the baking sun to collect the light they need, but they must also retain their moisture.

00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:49.280
Many do that by turning either their green stems or their waxy leaves into reservoirs.

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:59.280
This extraordinary cactus from Mexico is covered in thick white hair. It's called the Old Man.

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:12.280
This fur not only provides shade, but restricts the circulation of air around the surface of the stem, and that reduces water loss.

00:12:12.280 --> 00:12:19.280
In even hotter and drier climates, some plants avoid the sun altogether.

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:24.280
This is Fenestraria, the window plant.

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:32.280
It grows in the Kalahari Desert with most of its body buried beneath the soil.

00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:39.280
Only the blunt, flattened tips of its leaves are exposed.

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:54.280
Each contains tiny lenses which transmit the sunlight down through the vertical leaf onto its green photosynthesizing cells.

00:12:54.280 --> 00:13:03.280
Some desert plants that live in exceptionally hot, dry conditions take even more drastic measures.

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:14.280
Leaves are very thin and have a large surface area, so they lose a lot of moisture in heat, and many desert plants have done without them altogether.

00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:26.280
Instead, the valuable green pigment develops in the stems, and desert stems are often very thick and swollen, and that enables a plant to store water in them.

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:41.280
Not only that, but some desert plants have pleats in these stems, so that when there is a sudden rainstorm, those ridges can suck up all the moisture while it's there and expand in order to hold it.

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:51.280
The stems of some of these species swell to such a degree that they become almost spherical.

00:13:51.280 --> 00:14:01.280
A sphere has a minimum surface area for any given volume, and that enables the plant to hold as much water as possible.

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:08.280
This one is copiapoa from northern Chile, where it never rains at all.

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:13.280
But in fact, it doesn't need it.

00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:19.280
Under a microscope you can see why.

00:14:19.280 --> 00:14:32.280
There are thousands of tiny jagged structures on which the morning dew condenses so that the plant can collect it.

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:36.280
But the ultimate is this plant.

00:14:36.280 --> 00:14:41.280
It's hidden between these light-coloured pebbles.

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:47.280
Bloss feldia from Argentina.

00:14:47.280 --> 00:15:10.280
This is the only flowering plant that can withstand total desiccation for months on end, and then with a shower of rain or a little bit of water, it comes to life and produces a tiny little flower.

00:15:10.280 --> 00:15:21.280
This ability to wait for the right conditions is the trump card that enables many plants to survive in the dry zone.

00:15:21.280 --> 00:15:31.280
In many deserts, plants must endure months, sometimes years, of drought.

00:15:31.280 --> 00:15:40.280
They do it by slowing down their metabolism and going into a state of virtual dormancy.

00:15:40.280 --> 00:15:48.280
But when rains finally arrive, plants like these suddenly come to life.

00:15:48.280 --> 00:15:54.280
Their dried-out tissues absorb water like sponges.

00:15:54.280 --> 00:16:11.280
And once expanded, they use the moisture to grow.

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:21.280
This primitive, rootless plant from the Chihuahua Desert in Mexico is a spike moss, Selaginella.

00:16:21.280 --> 00:16:29.280
It's also called the resurrection plant because of its ability to seemingly come back from the dead.

00:16:29.280 --> 00:16:43.280
Rain starts a complex biochemical change in itself that enables it to ramp up its metabolism and grow very quickly while it can.

00:16:43.280 --> 00:16:52.280
But the most spectacular consequence of rain in the desert is an explosion of color.

00:17:13.280 --> 00:17:36.280
Desert flowers are as colorful as any in the world.

00:17:36.280 --> 00:17:54.280
They have to attract pollinators quickly before the deserts dry out once again.

00:17:54.280 --> 00:18:14.280
But moisture in the soil can evaporate almost as quickly as it arrives, and after a brief bloom, they die.

00:18:14.280 --> 00:18:23.280
Desert plants can only grow when the conditions are right, and that may be only for a few short periods in the year.

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:28.280
So many desert plants take a long time to reach full size.

00:18:28.280 --> 00:18:32.280
That agave there, for example, is over 40 years old.

00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:40.280
But during that time, it has manufactured food and gathered water and stored them in its great fat leaves.

00:18:40.280 --> 00:18:45.280
But it's not yet adult. This one is.

00:18:45.280 --> 00:18:53.280
This agave has started to withdraw that water and food from its leaves, so now they're beginning to crumple and wilt.

00:18:53.280 --> 00:19:03.280
And it uses that food to fuel the growth of this huge mast-like stem that grows from its center and will carry the flowers.

00:19:03.280 --> 00:19:12.280
And it reaches its full height at extraordinary speed.

00:19:12.280 --> 00:19:21.280
It can grow by a quarter of a meter every day.

00:19:21.280 --> 00:19:32.280
It reaches such a height that here in Kew's glass house, pains have to be removed from the roof to let it through.

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:43.280
This infrequency of flowering has given this species of agave a nickname, the century plant.

00:19:43.280 --> 00:19:48.280
So why does the agave produce this huge, tall mast?

00:19:48.280 --> 00:19:54.280
Well, some plants are pollinated by insects, and you attract insects with smell.

00:19:54.280 --> 00:20:02.280
But this plant is pollinated by birds, hummingbirds, and birds have little or no sense of smell.

00:20:02.280 --> 00:20:06.280
To attract them, you have to exploit their very sharp sight.

00:20:06.280 --> 00:20:19.280
You have to produce flowers that are very bright and put them right at the top of a prominent, tall mast.

00:20:19.280 --> 00:20:28.280
Growing at such a rate demands considerable effort.

00:20:28.280 --> 00:20:32.280
For the agave, it's terminal.

00:20:32.280 --> 00:20:38.280
The act of producing that mast is the last act in this plant's life.

00:20:38.280 --> 00:20:54.280
Having done it and been pollinated, it dies.

00:20:54.280 --> 00:20:58.280
Thanks to their extraordinary survival strategies,

00:20:58.280 --> 00:21:06.280
desert plants are able to thrive in places where others would die.

00:21:06.280 --> 00:21:14.280
But that makes them the focus of much unwanted attention.

00:21:14.280 --> 00:21:18.280
There's a downside to success for a desert plant.

00:21:18.280 --> 00:21:25.280
The more water it stores, the more tempting it is for a thirsty animal to try and steal it.

00:21:25.280 --> 00:21:34.280
So desert plants have to have good defenses.

00:21:34.280 --> 00:21:42.280
The techniques they use can tell us something about the herbivores that try to feed on them.

00:21:42.280 --> 00:21:55.280
This echinopsis from Argentina develops long, strong spines as a defense against large grazing animals, llamas.

00:21:55.280 --> 00:22:07.280
Such defenses also provide hiding places where an insect or a spider can keep out of harm's way.

00:22:07.280 --> 00:22:11.280
There are other ways in which a plant can protect itself.

00:22:11.280 --> 00:22:15.280
You can disguise yourself so you become virtually invisible.

00:22:15.280 --> 00:22:25.280
As these stone plants have done, these are not pebbles as you might think, they are living plants.

00:22:25.280 --> 00:22:34.280
These are lithops from the deserts of Africa, and their markings closely match their surroundings.

00:22:34.280 --> 00:22:45.280
They vary in color according to the rocks on which they grow, that's growing pale rocks and those on reddish rocks.

00:22:45.280 --> 00:22:55.280
Or you can load the water you contain with a particularly strong chemical, which animals might find distasteful.

00:22:55.280 --> 00:23:05.280
This is the Paioque plant from Mexico, and its sap not only puts off animals, but has the property of suppressing pain.

00:23:05.280 --> 00:23:20.280
So the local people use it for that purpose, and also in their religious rituals, for it's also hallucinogenic.

00:23:20.280 --> 00:23:30.280
In colder regions, pine trees also have to conserve water, for during the winter, water in the soil is frozen and so beyond their reach.

00:23:30.280 --> 00:23:38.280
Little evaporates from their leaves because they've been reduced to stiff, tough needles.

00:23:38.280 --> 00:23:48.280
Like cacti, they also produce distasteful chemicals.

00:23:48.280 --> 00:23:57.280
The distinctive smell that you sense when you walk among pine trees comes from volatile oils that the trees produce in their leaves.

00:23:57.280 --> 00:24:06.280
It's a form of defense, most insects can't bear it, but one can the pine aphid.

00:24:06.280 --> 00:24:12.280
When that attacks, however, the pine trees have a second line of defense.

00:24:12.280 --> 00:24:26.280
They produce that volatile oil from the cuts made by the insects in even greater quantity, and that crucially changes the nature of the smells around the trees.

00:24:26.280 --> 00:24:32.280
Aphid infestation triggers a complex chain reaction from the pines.

00:24:32.280 --> 00:24:42.280
Each time an aphid bites, the pine releases oily vapor from the wound, which fills the surrounding air.

00:24:42.280 --> 00:24:54.280
This triggers the release of further volatile from other branches, even other trees.

00:24:54.280 --> 00:25:10.280
The effect multiplies until eventually whole forests become cloaked in pine scent.

00:25:10.280 --> 00:25:34.280
This scent cloud attracts predatory ladybirds. They're as sensitive to it as sharks are to blood.

00:25:34.280 --> 00:25:44.280
This is the eyed ladybird, and it preys on pine aphids.

00:25:44.280 --> 00:26:08.280
It locates an infestation by following the trail of volatiles, and once there it makes short work of the prey.

00:26:08.280 --> 00:26:29.280
It's a mutually beneficial arrangement. The ladybird gets an easy meal, and the pine tree gets rid of a pest.

00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:40.280
Some dry zone plants use oils to defend themselves from other dangers, not from insects, but other plants.

00:26:40.280 --> 00:26:45.280
This is a eucalyptus, famous for the oil in its leaves.

00:26:45.280 --> 00:26:53.280
It produces that oil in such quantity that under certain conditions it can form a blue haze above the tree.

00:26:53.280 --> 00:27:02.280
In fact, the blue mountains of New South Wales and Australia get their name from the haze that hangs over the eucalyptus forest sometimes.

00:27:02.280 --> 00:27:08.280
When the leaves fall to the ground and rot, the oil leaches out into the soil.

00:27:08.280 --> 00:27:19.280
And there, reinforced by more oil secreted by the roots, it acts as an inhibitor that prevents other seeds of other plants from growing around its base,

00:27:19.280 --> 00:27:26.280
so reducing competition for the eucalyptus.

00:27:26.280 --> 00:27:38.280
Another species use oils in a different way, and a more dramatic one.

00:27:38.280 --> 00:27:50.280
If the temperature raises to 32 degrees centigrade, those substances in the leaves of this cystus plant from the Mediterranean can spontaneously burst into flame.

00:28:08.280 --> 00:28:21.280
The flames reduce the cystus to a cinder, and the plant itself will never recover.

00:28:21.280 --> 00:28:30.280
But this is not a disastrous accident. The cystus has evolved to burn.

00:28:30.280 --> 00:28:39.280
Such extraordinary sacrifice ensures a different kind of survival, survival of its offspring.

00:28:39.280 --> 00:28:46.280
The flames will also destroy all other plants from the surrounding area.

00:28:46.280 --> 00:29:06.280
And this gives the cystus a chance to extend its territory.

00:29:06.280 --> 00:29:17.280
From the ashes, a new generation of cystus quickly rises.

00:29:17.280 --> 00:29:24.280
Cystus seeds escape damage because they're enclosed in flame-resistant capsules.

00:29:24.280 --> 00:29:36.280
Furthermore, the smoke stimulates their germination.

00:29:36.280 --> 00:29:44.280
It has taken millions of years for such complex adaptations as this to evolve.

00:29:44.280 --> 00:29:55.280
But today, plants in the dry zone and beyond face a new threat.

00:29:55.280 --> 00:30:13.280
From rainforests to mountain ranges, the evidence is that plants across the world are disappearing in variety and number faster than at any other time in the Earth's history.

00:30:13.280 --> 00:30:23.280
Human beings are taking over more and more of the world's wild places.

00:30:23.280 --> 00:30:34.280
But human intervention can also be the key to their future survival.

00:30:34.280 --> 00:30:39.280
Q is at the forefront of the effort.

00:30:39.280 --> 00:30:51.280
Its unique facilities enable scientists here to both measure the loss of species and work out how to prevent extinctions before it's too late.

00:30:51.280 --> 00:30:57.280
And a crucial resource in that battle is held in this building.

00:30:57.280 --> 00:31:06.280
The herbarium.

00:31:06.280 --> 00:31:22.280
Its collection of preserved and dried specimens contains samples of 90% of all known plant species on Earth.

00:31:22.280 --> 00:31:29.280
Here, almost unbelievably, there are records of every single specimen ever sent to Q.

00:31:29.280 --> 00:31:32.280
It was founded in 1853.

00:31:32.280 --> 00:31:41.280
Now staff process about 50,000 specimens that are sent every year from all over the world.

00:31:41.280 --> 00:31:45.280
It contains about 8 million specimens.

00:31:45.280 --> 00:31:57.280
It's the biggest repository of botanical data in the world and Q's major weapon in the battle to save the plants of the planet.

00:31:57.280 --> 00:32:05.280
This vast data bank plays in monitoring the global health of plants.

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:08.280
It's run by curator David Mabillie.

00:32:08.280 --> 00:32:15.280
This herbarium, 8 million odd specimens, is actually a record of the vegetation of the Earth through time.

00:32:15.280 --> 00:32:22.280
There are some things which have gone completely extinct and so all that's known of them is just some herbarium specimens.

00:32:22.280 --> 00:32:32.280
For example, this is a relation of the olive, which was known only from one island off the coast of California and it hasn't been seen since 1873.

00:32:32.280 --> 00:32:36.280
Even then there were only three and that's all that we've got left now.

00:32:36.280 --> 00:32:41.280
Goodness. These specimens though are referred to, aren't they, for identification?

00:32:41.280 --> 00:32:43.280
Yes, well these are the standards.

00:32:43.280 --> 00:32:51.280
The ones which are in these red folders like this are actually the ones upon which the original description of a plant was made, the so-called type specimen.

00:32:51.280 --> 00:32:53.280
And therefore this is the kind of gold standard.

00:32:53.280 --> 00:33:00.280
If anybody really wants to know what is meant by a particular plant name, they must refer to the type specimen.

00:33:00.280 --> 00:33:07.280
The path to saving endangered species often begins here.

00:33:07.280 --> 00:33:17.280
For example, back in 1874, this specimen was sent back from the island of Rodriguez in the Indian Ocean.

00:33:17.280 --> 00:33:22.280
Of a tree they called the cafe marron, a kind of coffee.

00:33:22.280 --> 00:33:28.280
It was preserved here, given a Latin name and put in the herbarium.

00:33:28.280 --> 00:33:36.280
And then about 50 years later, people on Rodriguez realized that cafe marron had disappeared.

00:33:36.280 --> 00:33:43.280
Then in 1979, someone found a plant. Was it or was it not cafe marron?

00:33:43.280 --> 00:33:47.280
A specimen was sent here, compared with this specimen.

00:33:47.280 --> 00:33:50.280
And indeed it proved to be the case.

00:33:50.280 --> 00:33:59.280
So from that single plant, a cutting was taken and sent here to queue for propagation.

00:33:59.280 --> 00:34:06.280
From that single cutting, horticulturalists grew a handful of clone specimens.

00:34:06.280 --> 00:34:12.280
But although they flowered frequently, they never produced viable seed.

00:34:12.280 --> 00:34:19.280
Without the ability to reproduce, the cafe marron was still doomed.

00:34:19.280 --> 00:34:26.280
So the plant earned the nickname, the living dead.

00:34:26.280 --> 00:34:38.280
The daunting challenge of rescuing it was taken up by endangered plant specialist, Carlos Magdalena.

00:34:44.280 --> 00:34:52.280
After several months of experimentation, we found that the plant was still alive.

00:34:52.280 --> 00:35:02.280
After several months of experimentation with heat and light, he finally managed to induce the plant to produce seeds.

00:35:02.280 --> 00:35:12.280
But when they germinated, the young plants had leaves so unlike the cafe marron he knew, he thought he must have made a mistake.

00:35:12.280 --> 00:35:16.280
That's quite different from cafe marron.

00:35:16.280 --> 00:35:20.280
It's totally different, isn't it? Different color, different leaf shape.

00:35:20.280 --> 00:35:22.280
So were you surprised?

00:35:22.280 --> 00:35:27.280
Well, I was surprised in the way that I never seen them before.

00:35:27.280 --> 00:35:32.280
This strange plant looked nothing like the herbarium sample.

00:35:32.280 --> 00:35:39.280
At first, nobody could be sure that it was even the same species.

00:35:39.280 --> 00:35:42.280
But as it grew, it changed.

00:35:42.280 --> 00:35:49.280
And eventually it morphed into a recognizable adult plant.

00:35:49.280 --> 00:35:55.280
But what is the reason for this dramatic change as the cafe marron matures?

00:35:58.280 --> 00:36:02.280
The answer is camouflage.

00:36:03.280 --> 00:36:13.280
This is the most famous inhabitant of the island of Rodriguez, a giant tortoise.

00:36:13.280 --> 00:36:16.280
It loves bananas.

00:36:16.280 --> 00:36:18.280
Go on.

00:36:18.280 --> 00:36:24.280
But they also like green leaves and that's their main food.

00:36:27.280 --> 00:36:37.280
So any plant growing close to the ground has very little chance of survival while creatures like this are going around eating them.

00:36:37.280 --> 00:36:41.280
And that's the clue to the mystery of the cafe marron.

00:36:41.280 --> 00:36:49.280
When it's small, it produces leaves that are very thin and not even green.

00:36:49.280 --> 00:36:54.280
And the tortoise, whose eyesight is not very good, doesn't even see it.

00:36:54.280 --> 00:37:02.280
So he takes very little notice of leaves like this and the cafe marron can grow.

00:37:02.280 --> 00:37:13.280
As it grows, it becomes a bit bigger and still its leaves are thin and dark and rather inconspicuous.

00:37:13.280 --> 00:37:25.280
And it's not until it's really quite tall does the cafe marron plant produce its proper green leaves and its flowers.

00:37:25.280 --> 00:37:31.280
But by that time, it's well beyond the reach of a giant tortoise.

00:37:31.280 --> 00:37:35.280
So that is the solution to the cafe marron mystery.

00:37:35.280 --> 00:37:40.280
The plant produces two different leaves as a defense of being eaten.

00:37:40.280 --> 00:38:00.280
The cafe marron's survival is tribute to the skills developed here at Kew.

00:38:00.280 --> 00:38:06.280
But it is just one species.

00:38:06.280 --> 00:38:14.280
The ultimate aim is to safeguard all remaining plant species.

00:38:14.280 --> 00:38:21.280
And there may be a way of doing that by using these extraordinary structures.

00:38:21.280 --> 00:38:26.280
Seeds.

00:38:26.280 --> 00:38:43.280
A 3D microscope magnifies them up to 200 times and reveals how extraordinarily complex the surface of a seed can be.

00:38:43.280 --> 00:38:48.280
No two species are the same.

00:38:48.280 --> 00:38:56.280
These images have been given artificial colors, but many of their shapes are beyond explanation.

00:38:56.280 --> 00:38:59.280
Some structures, however, have a clear function.

00:38:59.280 --> 00:39:05.280
They help the seed to achieve one of its main purposes, to travel.

00:39:05.280 --> 00:39:09.280
Many seeds rely on animal couriers.

00:39:09.280 --> 00:39:18.280
The American stick seed attaches itself to the fur or skin of passing animals with hooks and barbed spikes.

00:39:18.280 --> 00:39:25.280
The amazing sticking structures of such a chikas inspired the invention of Velcro.

00:39:25.280 --> 00:39:41.280
Some seeds carry a little packet of nutritious fat, which persuades an ant to take it back to its underground nest, where the seed, having surrendered its fatty reward to the ants, can germinate in safety.

00:39:41.280 --> 00:39:47.280
The seeds of orchids are minuscule and produced in their millions.

00:39:47.280 --> 00:39:51.280
They blow away like dust.

00:39:51.280 --> 00:40:03.280
In contrast, this much bigger seed has a large flat membrane which serves as the wing so that it can float great distances on the wind.

00:40:03.280 --> 00:40:10.280
Other wind dispersed seeds, like the yellow paintbrush, have a honeycomb texture.

00:40:10.280 --> 00:40:22.280
Such complex sculpturing enables seeds to catch the slightest wind current and ride thermals, traveling long distances from their mother plants.

00:40:22.280 --> 00:40:30.280
But these images also reveal the incredible toughness of the seed's outer capsule.

00:40:30.280 --> 00:40:35.280
This strength may be the key to the survival of all plants.

00:40:35.280 --> 00:40:41.280
For seeds can survive all kinds of hardship.

00:40:41.280 --> 00:40:46.280
This plant is called the pincushion and grows in South Africa.

00:40:46.280 --> 00:40:53.280
And this individual specimen is something of a miracle.

00:40:53.280 --> 00:41:02.280
In 1803, a British man-of-war captured a Dutch merchantman coming back from the Cape of Good Hope.

00:41:02.280 --> 00:41:06.280
And on board in the cargo, they found a packet of seeds.

00:41:06.280 --> 00:41:12.280
When they got back to Britain, the seeds went to the Tower of London for quite a long time.

00:41:12.280 --> 00:41:16.280
But eventually they found their way here to Kew.

00:41:16.280 --> 00:41:21.280
And one of them germinated and produced this plant.

00:41:21.280 --> 00:41:31.280
After 200 years, it just shows how long seeds can survive, even without help.

00:41:31.280 --> 00:41:47.280
This ability to endure has inspired Kew's latest groundbreaking project.

00:41:47.280 --> 00:41:56.280
Its ultimate goal is to ensure the survival of every remaining species of plant on Earth.

00:41:56.280 --> 00:42:09.280
The work takes place deep underground in a labyrinth of sealed vaults that are encased in steel and concrete.

00:42:09.280 --> 00:42:15.280
It's called the Kew Millennium Seed Bank.

00:42:15.280 --> 00:42:24.280
Its contents are so valuable that it was specifically designed to withstand the impact of a bomb or an air crash.

00:42:24.280 --> 00:42:29.280
Inside these vaults, there are seeds gathered from plants all over the world.

00:42:29.280 --> 00:42:57.280
In fact, as much as 10% of the world's known species are represented in there.

00:42:57.280 --> 00:43:08.280
The heart of the Seed Bank project is this gigantic freezer full of seeds in sealed jars.

00:43:08.280 --> 00:43:15.280
The conditions inside are perfect for long-term storage.

00:43:15.280 --> 00:43:21.280
But the job is aided by the very nature of seeds.

00:43:21.280 --> 00:43:33.280
The seed is the stage in which a plant can remain dormant, waiting until conditions for germination arrive.

00:43:33.280 --> 00:43:37.280
Inside each seed, there's a store of food.

00:43:37.280 --> 00:43:43.280
Outside, there's a tough protective shell to guard against predation or damage.

00:43:43.280 --> 00:43:51.280
In fact, a seed is a kind of time capsule that can live for sometimes a decade, sometimes even centuries.

00:43:51.280 --> 00:43:56.280
The aim of the Seed Bank is to prolong that period as long as possible.

00:43:56.280 --> 00:44:08.280
To do that, they clean the seeds, dry them, put them in jars like this, and then keep them here at minus 20 degrees centigrade.

00:44:08.280 --> 00:44:13.280
How long they will survive under these conditions, nobody knows.

00:44:13.280 --> 00:44:20.280
But it's certainly going to be for a very long time.

00:44:20.280 --> 00:44:26.280
New samples arrive at the Seed Bank every day.

00:44:26.280 --> 00:44:35.280
They are first identified and studied in microscopic detail by chief morphologist Dr Wolfgang Stuppi.

00:44:35.280 --> 00:44:47.280
The latest report from Israel claims that date palm seeds have been found in King Herod's, the great palace in Masada, and they germinated after 2,000 years.

00:44:47.280 --> 00:44:59.280
So that is possible, and this is the principle the Millennium Seed Bank is built upon, that seeds can survive for millennia if necessary.

00:44:59.280 --> 00:45:03.280
The longevity of most kinds of seeds is unknown.

00:45:03.280 --> 00:45:12.280
So they must be regularly checked.

00:45:12.280 --> 00:45:38.280
Samples of seeds are periodically removed from the vaults and germinated.

00:45:38.280 --> 00:45:48.280
If most grow, the whole batch can be left in the vaults for another decade.

00:45:48.280 --> 00:46:06.280
But sometimes a sample fails, like these anemone seeds which, although germinating in sterilised conditions, become choked with fungus.

00:46:06.280 --> 00:46:18.280
When this happens, the scientists must find out why and how to prevent it happening again.

00:46:18.280 --> 00:46:28.280
The Seed Bank project is still in its infancy, but the evidence is that there's no time to lose.

00:46:28.280 --> 00:46:34.280
There are already seeds stored here from plants that are now extinct in the wild.

00:46:34.280 --> 00:46:43.280
The problem is, what many people don't understand, is that if a species goes extinct, it means it is gone for us forever.

00:46:43.280 --> 00:46:47.280
There were five mass extinctions in the Earth's history.

00:46:47.280 --> 00:46:55.280
After every mass extinction, it took between four and 20 million years for biodiversity to bounce back to pre-extinction levels.

00:46:55.280 --> 00:47:02.280
Now, clearly we cannot wait four million years for life to bounce back.

00:47:02.280 --> 00:47:14.280
So if we don't keep the seeds here and this plant species goes extinct, it will be lost to mankind forever.

00:47:14.280 --> 00:47:20.280
Thankfully, there's a great momentum behind the Seed Bank project.

00:47:20.280 --> 00:47:33.280
Over the next few decades, these shelves will be filled to capacity.

00:47:33.280 --> 00:47:42.280
There's enough space here to hold seed from every single species of plants on this planet.

00:47:42.280 --> 00:47:48.280
But this place is not just an insurance policy against the ultimate apocalypse.

00:47:48.280 --> 00:47:51.280
This is of huge value right now.

00:47:51.280 --> 00:48:00.280
For wherever we discover that a plant is teetering on the brink of extinction, we can now boast of its numbers with seeds from here,

00:48:00.280 --> 00:48:06.280
and so ensure that no plant species on Earth need go extinct.

00:48:06.280 --> 00:48:23.280
There are an estimated 400,000 different species of plant on the planet.

00:48:23.280 --> 00:48:30.280
We can't possibly understand the lives of each and every one of them.

00:48:30.280 --> 00:48:44.280
But here at the Royal Botanic Gardens in Kew, one of the birthplaces of scientific botany, we've begun to glimpse their extraordinary world.

00:48:44.280 --> 00:49:01.280
Kew is still in the forefront of botanical research, helping to ensure that we not only understand plants, but protect them in all their astonishing variety.

00:49:01.280 --> 00:49:14.280
And here we can see how plants in all habitats and environments are intricately connected with animals and with fungi.

00:49:14.280 --> 00:49:32.280
Not only do plants give the world much of its beauty, they are indeed the very basis of all life on Earth.
